index c0eedc1..fb2dbc2 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -1018,6 +1018,83 @@ conductor process stopped writing; and a directory with neither file is reported
 as `incomplete`. `silent` is a flag for the operator, nothing more: conductor never
 modifies, moves, reclaims, or deletes a run directory, and nothing is reclaimed.
 
+### Structured error kinds
+
+`Result.failure()` reduces a dispatch to one line of prose, but "a rate limit"
+and "a broken gate" can read almost the same in prose while calling for
+opposite responses: retry the first, escalate on the second. `errors.py`
+classifies every not-ok result into exactly one `kind`, checked in this fixed
+order (first match wins, so a cap kill that also timed out is `cap`, and a
+fleet error that mentions a rate limit is `rate_limit`, not `fleet_error`):
+
+`interrupted`, `cancelled`, `cap`, `breaker`, `timeout`, `setup`, `refused`,
+`rate_limit`, `transport`, `refusal`, `fleet_error`, `exit`, `gate`, `no_op`,
+`read_moved_bytes`, `no_answer`, `commit`, `unknown`.
+
+`interrupted` and `cancelled` are conductor's own stop signals; `cap` covers
+a budget exceeded, a cap unenforced (the run came back unpriced against a
+cap), and a watcher's cap kill; `breaker` is any of the four progress
+breakers tripping; `setup` is a `setup` command that failed or timed out;
+`refused` is conductor declining to spawn at all (an isolation failure, a
+ports or include refusal, a non-repo write target, and the rest of
+`_refused_result`'s callers) -- `spawned: false` with an error set.
+`rate_limit`, `transport`, and `refusal` are read from the fleet's own error
+text (and, for `refusal`, Claude's `subtype`) against a per-fleet substring
+table:
+
+| kind | fleet | substrings | seen |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `rate_limit` | any | `rate limit`, `rate_limit`, `429`, `overloaded`, `529`, `quota`, `resource exhausted`, `too many requests` | Claude budget/rate stops, Codex/Antigravity/Cursor 429s, Anthropic 529 "overloaded", Google "resource exhausted" |
+| `transport` | any | `ECONNRESET`, `ECONNREFUSED`, `ETIMEDOUT`, `EPIPE`, `socket hang up`, `fetch failed`, `network`, `502`, `503`, `504` | node/http transport failures reported by any fleet's own error text |
+| `transport` | `claude` | `stream ended without a result event` | a Claude stream cut short with no final result event |
+| `refusal` | any | `I can't help`, `I cannot help` | the model declining the task outright, verbatim |
+| `refusal` | `claude` | any `subtype` starting `error_` (other than `error_max_budget_usd` and `error_max_turns`) whose text also says `refus`, `cannot help`, or `not able to` | Claude's own refusal subtypes |
+
+`fleet_error` is any other fleet-reported failure; `exit` a nonzero exit with
+none; `gate` a gate (or its clean replacement) that ran and did not pass;
+`no_op` a write that moved no bytes; `read_moved_bytes` a read dispatch that
+moved bytes anyway; `no_answer` a read dispatch with no answer; `commit` a
+requested commit that did not land; `unknown` is anything else. `error_kind`
+is computed from a `Result` or a plain receipt dict, never stored as its own
+source of truth, so an old receipt read back still classifies correctly.
+Every receipt (`Result.to_dict()`, `Result.summary()`) carries `kind`, and
+`conductor runs` shows it beside each row.
+
+A lane's `fallback` entries may set `"on": [<kind>, ...]` -- the fallback
+only runs when the attempt right before it in the same lane failed with one
+of these kinds; omitted (the default, and every primary and cascade attempt,
+which may never set it) means every kind, as before an attempt that is
+passed over notes `skipped fallback <label>: does not handle <kind>` on the
+lane, and the walk continues to the one after. An unknown kind is refused at
+load, naming the lane and the fallback. The lane's receipt carries `kinds`:
+the kind of every attempt dispatched, in order (`null` for one that
+succeeded), and each attempt row carries its own `kind`.
+
+A mission may also set `"retry"` to retry the *same* attempt before falling
+back to the next one:
+
+```json
+{"retry": {"kinds": ["rate_limit", "transport"], "attempts": 2, "backoff_s": 5}}
+```
+
+`kinds` defaults to `["rate_limit", "transport"]`, `attempts` (1-5) is how
+many extra tries an attempt gets, and `backoff_s` (default 0) is the base of
+an exponential backoff (`backoff_s * 2**i`) slept, in one-second steps so a
+stop request or the lane's own cancel event ends it exactly like a running
+dispatch would, between tries. A retry is a fresh dispatch (a new run id) of
+the same spec, under the same cancel event and ledger rules as the attempt
+it repeats; its row carries `retry_of` (the first try's run id) and `retry`
+(which retry this is). A dry run never retries.
+
+`report.md` shows one line when any lane failed: `Errors: <kind> x<n>, ...`,
+and each failed attempt's row in a lane's own section shows its kind next to
+its error text. `MissionResult.errors` (`result.json`, `conductor missions`)
+is `{"<kind>": <count>}` over every attempt of every lane, empty when nothing
+failed.
+
+Evidence (`docs/ROADMAP-2026-09.md` item C5): the audit's own top capability
+ask, and the OpenAI Agents SDK's `error_handlers`.
+
 ## Commands
 
 - `conductor fleets`: the routing policy, and whether each binary is installed
